      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2053943340626858129",
      "url": "https://x.com/WWIIMemorial/status/2053943340626858129",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/WWIIMemorial/status/2053943340626858129",
      "text": "U.S. Army Sergeant John Randolph McKinney of Woodcliff, Georgia, was awarded  the Medal of Honor for his extraordinary actions on May 11, 1945, in Luzon, the Philippines.\n\nMcKinney enlisted in the Army in November 1942 from Screven County, Georgia. By May 11th, 1945, he was serving as a private in Company A, 123rd Infantry, 33rd Infantry Division. US Army Pvt. John McKinney had stood guard duty and had just gone to his tent in the early hours of May 11, 1945, on the island of Luzon, Philippines. The vanguard of a Japanese force slipped past the guard post. Sgt. Fukutaro Morii threw McKinney's tent flap open and slashed down with his sword to minimize the sound of the undetected attack. He severed part of McKinney's ear. McKinney, a skilled hunter from Georgia, grabbed the rifle he slept with, bashed Morii in the chin and finished him off with another blow to the head.\n\nOver the next 36 minutes, McKinney protected the flank of his company and his sleeping comrades by killing 38 of the enemy. McKinney did so through point-blank, kill-or-be-killed encounters and rapid-fire, accurate shots with various M1 rifles he picked up and fired at charging enemies. Early in the engagement, he returned to his foxhole, where he eliminated first one wave and then part of the second wave of the main attack force. Several in the second wave made it to the foxhole, where McKinney first shot and then clubbed his assailants in hand-to-hand combat.\n\nHe was awarded the Medal of Honor for his actions on this day. McKinney died in April 1997 and is buried at Double Heads Baptist Church in Sylvania, Georgia.\n\n#WeRememberThem",
